WEBVTT

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:13.180
 In those days, nobody knew how to grow strawberries organically on any scale in a way that paid all the bills for the strawberries.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:21.020
 So we took a little piece of land that was isolated from the rest of the fields and created this randomized trial

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:28.980
 and did organic treatment and conventional treatments right next to each other, then measured everything really carefully.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:37.370
 Steve did all the measuring, and I kept track of the numbers on the harvest, because we did see some differences in production.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:42.180
 But the differences were not anywhere near as much as people expected.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:48.980
 And the price differential in the organic price, which had they been sold organically, would have made up for the difference.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:55.180
 You know, it’s good to have somebody around who’s saying, "Well, you know, I think it’'ll work, you know."

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:03.920
 Because a lot of people were saying, "No, it’ wouldn’ work, you know, because I’'d worked in the conventional industry, and they all said, "You can grow them all right, but you can’'t make a living at it."

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.200
 But actually, they looked beautiful, and they tasted great, and I got enough to pay all the bills.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:15.580
 Like, so right now, I’'m, you know, one half of a percent of the organic strawberries in California.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:23.800
 And I’'m proud of that, you know. I’'m just, that’'s fine. I don’ need to be a big one.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:32.100
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I’'m Chelsea Wills.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:38.370
 This season, we’'re talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:43.560
 These people have shaped the food movement in California. We’ talk with a diverse group of

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.760
 California’'s rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens,

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:54.440
 and communities that reshape the way we think about food. This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea

00:01:54.440 --> 00:02:00.120
 Wills. Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:07.140
 making this season possible. Inspired by the United Farm Workers' struggle for justice and an emerging

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:13.700
 environmental movement, Jim Cochran started Swanton Berry Farm in 1983 on four leased acres on the

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.420
 California coast. At the time, everyone said it was impossible to make a living growing organic

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:24.380
 strawberries. But after some creative experimentation and a collaboration with agroecologist Steve

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:30.060
 Gleesman and Sean Sweezy at UC Santa Cruz, Swanton became the first certified organic strawberry

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:35.660
 farm in California. As the organic berry industry boomed in California, Jim kept implementing his

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:41.340
 vision for a more sustainable agriculture, signing a contract with the United Farm Workers that covers

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:47.580
 all of his full-time employees and offering them a stock ownership program. He aims to set an example with

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:53.260
 his commercially viable, socially just, and sustainable farming business. Jim is currently in the process

00:02:53.260 --> 00:03:00.140
 of retiring from Swanton Berry Farm and working on the food commons, a model for supporting local food

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.420
 systems with a pilot food hub in Fresno, California.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:10.330
 Jim Cochran: This is Jim Cochran. We're sitting at the farm stand of Swanton Berry Farm. It's an old building that was

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:17.170
 brought here after World War II to house farm workers from the Bracero program. And there are

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:23.730
 buildings like this all up and down the coast, wherever there are farms. They're all exactly the

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:29.600
 same. Essentially, what they are is old army barracks. In fact, I lived in an army barracks just like

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:36.960
 this when I was in the army. And so what we've done is we've converted this building into a little,

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:43.510
 what we call a farm stand, where we have products that we grow and things that we make out of the

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:48.980
 products that we grow. We don't sell anything that we don't grow. And we also have a commercial kitchen

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:56.150
 here. So we make pies and strawberry shortcake and jam and so forth. Everything's with the union labor,

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:03.110
 both the field production workers and the kitchen people who make the pies and so forth, as are all

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:09.480
 the delivery people and all the people who work at the farmer's market as well. So we have a fully

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:17.590
 union labor force here. It consists of about, I'd say, 25 permanent full-time and then probably about

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:25.020
 10 part-time people who work at the UPIC or do a farmer's market one or two days a week. Farmer's

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:34.200
 market is a really grueling job and it really wears people out. And doing two farmer's markets a week is

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:43.140
 a lot for a person. Jim, when I think of Swanton Berry Farm, I think of really good berries and also

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:50.260
 about kind of leading the way in organics and the ability to produce organic produce in California and

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:56.820
 also labor solidarity. So I want to talk about all of those, but which of those for you personally,

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.280
 which of those came first?

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:08.080
 It was really the interest in labor issues for farmworkers and former sharecroppers. Back in 1970,

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:14.800
 I was a small part of a small team that organized the first farmworker owned strawberry farm in

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:20.590
 California as a cooperative. And that really got me interested in the business. And of course,

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:26.350
 at those days, everything was all conventional. And what we were trying to do was bring the ability of

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:32.590
 people who had worked on strawberry farms in ownership of their own farms. And that worked

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:38.830
 reasonably well. But the cooperative structure was difficult because the fortunes of the co-ops would

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:45.050
 go up and down tremendously. And they were very thinly capitalized. And so all it took was a couple

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:46.930
 bad years and bingo, they were gone.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:53.410
 And so what wound up happening is some of the people who worked in the co-ops went on to start their own farms.

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:59.810
 And in fact, today, some of the people who are farming in the Watsonville area, either their parents or

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:07.050
 themselves or even their grandparents were involved in one of these co-ops that were basically a phenomenon of

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:14.570
 the 1970s and very early 1980s. But at that time, I was also interested in organic farming.

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:21.050
 And as far as I knew, no one in California had actually operated a strawberry farm that was organic.

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:27.050
 There were people who were vegetable farmers and who had a little bit of strawberries here and there.

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:31.450
 But there wasn't anybody who was really, you know, what we call a strawberry farm.

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:38.330
 The way the industry works, in part because strawberries are so complicated to grow successfully,

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:44.810
 people are, they're just strawberry farmers. And they may grow some other things, but that's what they

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:50.010
 define themselves as strawberry farmers. So there, at that point, there was no one in the state who says,

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:56.490
 well, I'm a strawberry farmer and I grow organically. There was up in Washington State, Gene Kahn had

00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:02.330
 Gascadian Farm, and he was growing strawberries organically from, oh God, in the 70s, really a long time ago.

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:08.810
 And, but they were for processing. I think Dale Koch and some others had grown a little bit of

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:15.380
 strawberries, but it wasn't on the scale that I did. Now, of course, at this point, Dale's got,

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:22.920
 you know, many times more strawberries than I do. And so does everybody else. But in those days,

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:30.390
 there was really nobody knew how to grow strawberries organically on any scale in a way that paid all

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:36.820
 the bills for the strawberries. Nobody had really figured out how to grow, you know, five or ten acres

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:42.360
 of strawberries and actually make a career out of it, make a living out of growing strawberries. And so

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:49.330
 that's the point at which I decided to go out on my own and try to figure out how to do that. So I spent

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:55.270
 about five or six years trying out different varieties and different growing methods and so

00:07:55.270 --> 00:08:02.680
 forth and drawing heavily on the knowledge of organic farming that was really, there was a lot of it

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:07.240
 here in the Central Coast area. A lot of people knew how to grow, you know, a whole bunch of different

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:13.610
 crops. And so the commitment to organic came after my commitment to labor issues, because I really felt

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:22.660
 like the labor issues were not being addressed by the organic movement at all. And so I said,

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:27.370
 OK, well, I'm going to try to just be a good employer for a while. I did that for a number of

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:36.220
 years. But then the United Farm Workers began organizing in Watsonville. Their goal was to bring higher

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:42.780
 labor standards to the strawberry industry in general. So I said, well, you know, maybe I should talk to the

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:48.860
 union and my employees should talk to the union also. And maybe we could come up with some sort

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:56.920
 of a contract that would work for all of us, but would also help raise the discussion of labor issues

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:03.720
 in the organic world, because I felt that that was really important. I was a small potato person in the

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:11.300
 organic movement. And I felt like it wasn't my job to tell other people what to do with their farming

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:16.980
 operations of whatever size. And so if I, you know, really believed in all of this stuff, I should just

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.960
 do it myself and just say, well, this is what I believe. This is what I'm going to do. And so I did.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:27.400
 I signed a contract with the United Farm Workers in 1998. You know, it was expensive. It costs a lot

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:33.720
 more. Today, my costs are quite a bit higher than other people's in the business. But I'm still in

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:41.160
 business and I'm still profitable. I mean, I do have years when it's when it's not profitable. But I have

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:48.130
 other years that make up for it. Basically, my goal has been to show that it's possible to pay higher

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:54.690
 wages, pay all of your state required things like workers comp and unemployment and Social Security and

00:09:54.690 --> 00:10:04.060
 so forth, plus pay the union benefits, namely health insurance, dental insurance, pension plan, vacation

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:11.290
 pay, and holiday pay. So it's quite a substantial package. At this point, it's about a $3 an hour

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:18.300
 package on top of the wages and so forth. In addition to that, we provide housing, inexpensive housing

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:26.210
 people who work for us. So they're able to save money when they work here.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:31.350
 Working with the United Farm Workers is, it's not a normal agribusiness move. And it's also not

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:37.970
 really a normal, a common organic. I remember one of the first times I had your strawberries was

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:44.050
 buying them at the Ferry Plaza market at your stand, flying the UFW, the red and black UFW flags.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:47.570
 So there's a, and at that time, you know, 10 years ago, it felt like a

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:50.450
 surprising site in the middle of a Ferry Plaza farmer's market.

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:57.570
 Well, it was not a popular move among the, either the conventional strawberry world or the organic

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:06.140
 world. But I felt like, you know, it was my duty to the cause of, you know, improving the working

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:11.500
 conditions of farm workers that I do that. I just didn't feel right, you know, being concerned about

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:17.660
 the environment and about, you know, my customers and so forth. And that also being concerned about the,

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:22.440
 people who did the work in the fields. And furthermore, I felt like it was important to

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:28.920
 be certified in that. And for example, you know, in the organic world, they, they, you know,

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:34.580
 some people say, well, I am organic, but I just don't, I'm not certified. Well, you know,

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:40.010
 that we all believe pretty strongly that being certified is very important. And you can't just

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:46.370
 say, Oh, I'm organic. And because a lot of stuff can slide that isn't truly organic. You never really

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:53.820
 know. And it was the same thing with labor. You can say, Oh, well, I do, you know, a good job. We treat

00:11:53.820 --> 00:12:01.140
 everybody like family. And, well, that's, that's sort of a good claim and may be true. But, you know,

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:07.250
 then of course, it may just be not true. And since there was no interest in the organic community in

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:15.720
 actually having a labor certification program, I felt like the union route was a, was a good one.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:21.480
 The other good thing about the union thing is that it, you know, it's an outside check on the claims

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:25.720
 that you make because the union, you know, if you're saying you're doing something when you're not,

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:32.190
 they're going to talk to you about it. And so it's a way for workers to have a voice. And furthermore,

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:36.750
 my experience in the co-ops was that the co-ops were terrible employers. In general,

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:43.170
 the people who work on the co-op farms had worse working conditions than they did working for some

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:49.740
 of the large agribusiness farms. And so that really struck me. And people here, they say, Oh,

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:55.200
 co-op, you know, Oh, it must be wonderful. You know? No, it was not wonderful for the workers.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:13:01.400
 So you have to understand there's, if you have the, you have the farmers on their little plot,

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:07.360
 two or three acre plot of strawberries, they have a voice, right? Because they're a member of the co-op,

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:13.710
 but the people who work for them have no voice. And so, you know, there's no regulation of how they're

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:19.470
 paid or whether they're paid correctly and whether there's a securities being paid, all that stuff is

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:31.450
 just not taken care of. So I felt like that the union was more of a first step, you know, the whole time.

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:37.100
 And I tell people, it's sort of like, are you buying a new car? That's got a guarantee that,

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:42.740
 you know, everything works. Well, are you going to buy an old junk car and try to keep it going?

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:47.940
 Well, you know, I felt like having a new contract, it's expensive, but you get what you pay for.

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:53.460
 And so people are really totally into working here and we don't really have quality control issues

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:58.950
 because everybody, you know, wants to do a good job and they do do a good job. And it's important for

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:04.070
 the public to ask other people what they're doing on their farms. Cause I mean, after all,

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:11.270
 I mean, most organic food is produced by Mexican immigrant farm workers. So I think it's a fair

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:16.150
 question to ask, well, you know, how are they getting paid or are people paying their social security,

00:14:16.150 --> 00:14:21.350
 their workers comp, their unemployment insurance, you know, all that stuff is important. You know,

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:25.810
 that's what I feel we should, everybody should be doing the right thing, following the rules.

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:35.320
 The union labor. I know that Cesar Chavez talked about,

00:14:35.320 --> 00:15:05.810
 you know, you figured, well, this is part of the business and, you know, it just,

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:12.260
 you get over it and, um, careful as you can, you know, but you, it's sort of inevitable that you get

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:17.950
 some exposures. Now things are better now than they used to be actually quite a bit better. I think

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:22.990
 everybody's a lot more careful. I mean, everybody in the conventional world is a lot more careful than

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:28.680
 they used to be, than we used to be back 35 years ago, because people know more about it. And I think

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:35.000
 California farmers are in a much higher standard than people in Mexico or other places. So, you know,

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:44.110
 California farmers do a good job, much better job than we did 30 years ago of, um, you know,

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:49.320
 following the rules and the rules are difficult to follow. But at any rate, you know, people,

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:53.960
 the public doesn't really understand how much money it costs to follow all the rules. I mean,

00:15:53.960 --> 00:16:01.050
 there's the fish that's really expensive for farmers, water issues. Oh my gosh. I mean,

00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:06.570
 you should see how much money we spend on workers comp, at least a couple of hundred thousand dollars

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:17.200
 a year on workers comp. It's a lot of money. So there's this point in the eighties and the early

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:23.720
 eighties where, where you are experimenting with organic berries. People are telling you it's not

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:31.290
 possible to do on a commercial scale. And then you team up with a researcher who I know really well,

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:37.850
 um, because he was my PhD thesis advisor, Steve Gleesman and the collaboration that started

00:16:37.850 --> 00:16:44.160
 then has kind of become a legend of what an organic conversion is or what farmer research

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:50.170
 collaborations can be. So can, can you talk about kind of the early, the early part of that

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:51.610
 collaboration, how that got started?

00:16:51.610 --> 00:17:01.100
 Gleesman happened to live next door across the field from my strawberry field.

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:07.960
 And so we started talking and I would go and scratch my head and say, well, I'm trying to figure this

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:14.570
 out and trying to figure that out. And, um, he had some ideas of course, and, um, I'd been reading

00:17:14.570 --> 00:17:21.830
 some things about what perhaps they had done 50 years ago or a hundred years ago for soil health in

00:17:21.830 --> 00:17:27.350
 general. And, and then for insect, I don't know if you can use the word management, but they're all

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:33.350
 out there and yeah, it's not management, but it's, um, going with the flow somehow in a way that doesn't

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:44.130
 put you out of business. And, uh, it was very helpful to have Steve, one for the scientific

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:49.820
 reason. But secondly, for, to have somebody saying, yeah, come on, you can do it, you know?

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:57.090
 And that's, you know, cause boy, when it was like out there on my own, I mean, it was like,

00:17:57.090 --> 00:18:02.620
 you know, it's rough cause you've got all your money in this crop and it could get wiped out.

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:10.960
 And, you know, sometimes parts of it did. And, you know, I had no subsidies, no government money,

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:20.000
 nothing. And, um, so it was, um, scary, you know, it's good to have somebody around who's saying,

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:26.010
 well, you know, I think it'll work, you know, and cause a lot of people were saying, no, it wouldn't

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:33.360
 work. You know, a lot of people in the industry, cause I still had friends in the industry, you know,

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:38.860
 cause I, I'd worked in the conventional industry and they all said, no, you can grow them all right,

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:43.980
 but you can't make a living at it. You can't get enough yield or they won't look nice. And,

00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:49.380
 you know, but actually they look beautiful and they tasted great. And I got enough to pay all

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:55.950
 the bills. And so you set up a small experiment, right? Is it, it's a little corner of the field and

00:18:55.950 --> 00:19:08.710
 look at what I was doing from a scientific perspective. So I, we took a little piece

00:19:08.710 --> 00:19:17.120
 of land that was isolated from the rest of the fields and, um, created this randomized trial and did

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:25.300
 organic treatment and conventional treatments right next to each other, then measured everything really

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:32.550
 carefully. Steve did all the measuring and I kept track of the numbers on the harvest because we kept

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:39.450
 each plot separate. And of course it wasn't really organic because it was right next to a conventional

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:46.230
 treatment. And it was, it was separated by a plastic baffle, but you know, we didn't sell it as organic.

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:53.680
 But on the other hand, um, we did see some differences in production, but the differences were

00:19:53.680 --> 00:20:00.390
 not anywhere near as much as people expected and the price differential in the organic price, which had

00:20:00.390 --> 00:20:07.890
 they been sold organically would have made up for the difference. And so that was a sort of a turning

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:15.420
 point. Um, we held a field day and, and a bunch of conventional growers came to the field day and saw the,

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:24.210
 what we were doing. And of course, you know, they had better soil and better equipment and more money.

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:29.820
 And, um, some of them picked it up and said, well, gee, you know, if Cochran can do this,

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:36.450
 I can do this and I can do it even better. And it took a few more years for them to see

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:42.170
 me actually be in the marketplace and come by the field and see the field and say, well, you know,

00:20:42.170 --> 00:20:47.990
 it seems to be working for him. And so then there was a point at which more people

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:53.250
 started actually trying a little plot of organic strawberries of the conventional

00:20:53.250 --> 00:21:01.860
 growers. Um, and so now most growers are, you know, many of them do, you know, like,

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:08.610
 so right now I'm, you know, one half of a percent of the organic strawberries in California.

00:21:10.330 --> 00:21:15.940
 And I'm proud of that. You know, I'm just, um, that's fine. I don't need to be a big one.

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:19.620
 I made a decision a long time ago, not to get big in part because,

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:26.870
 you know, when I was farming down in Salinas, you know, we had a 200 acre ranch. It was a big

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:33.310
 operation. And the, sometimes the old timers would come by and say, oh God, I remember when I only had

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:39.870
 200 acres and my life was much better than it is now when I've got 8,000 acres, you know,

00:21:40.990 --> 00:21:46.670
 and I've got, you know, $20 million line of credit and I, I'm totally stressed all the time and blah,

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:53.690
 blah, blah. And there's something to be said for just staying small. And then furthermore,

00:21:53.690 --> 00:22:01.280
 I could control quality a lot better. It's tough to do really good quality control on a, on a large

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:07.030
 operation. It was sort of a personal decision that I made. And so now we lease, oh gosh,

00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:18.320
 about 125 acres or so, maybe 130, but we only have water for almost a hundred acres. And about 14 of

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:24.370
 those acres are in strawberries. You know, one out of seven acres that we have under cultivation is

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:33.370
 in strawberries. And we rotate other crops around and try not to come back on the same ground for

00:22:33.370 --> 00:22:43.170
 for five years. Longer would be better, but some of the other crops that we grow are, are perennials

00:22:43.170 --> 00:22:46.900
 like, like bushberries and artichokes and so forth.

00:22:46.900 --> 00:22:51.540
 And what were some of the problems that came up or what were some of the things that you experimented

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:56.810
 with when you were developing the set of techniques that, of organic farming?

00:22:56.810 --> 00:23:06.440
 Well, interestingly enough, that particular plot didn't have many problems. One of the problems

00:23:06.440 --> 00:23:15.850
 that I had was thinking that I understood what was happening, imagining that I understood what was

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:22.490
 going on in the soil, what was going on with the insects. Because there was, I didn't understand it,

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:27.970
 you know, and it was just too complex. So I'd make this and make some intervention and it would screw

00:23:27.970 --> 00:23:34.180
 something else up. And, uh, you know, it just, it was better to take a few steps back and then just

00:23:34.180 --> 00:23:44.460
 aim for general soil nutrition and not try to dose some magic thing. I remember one time I thought,

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:52.050
 oh boy, fish meal would be really good. And so I dug the holes that we were going to plant in and

00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:58.030
 put some fish meal in the bottom and, and then put, you know, an inch and a half of soil on top and

00:23:58.030 --> 00:24:04.370
 then planted the transplant. And I'll be darned if it didn't burn the plants, you know, and I should

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:12.190
 have just mixed a little bit in with the compost and left it at that. So that's what I, I just went

00:24:12.190 --> 00:24:19.990
 back to general good, good farming practices that have been around for 10,000 years, you know,

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:27.830
 rotate the crops, um, grow a cover crop when you can. And in the old times they used to add just

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:33.910
 manure, but we added compost, you know, that's handled in a way that makes it safe. And then I

00:24:33.910 --> 00:24:40.910
 had just, you know, some crops were good in the rotation and other crops weren't. And it turns out

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:49.540
 that the, um, brassica crops were good, um, help reduce the amount of verticillium in the soil.

00:24:49.540 --> 00:24:55.650
 Um, oddly enough, when I was in Salinas, the farm advisor told me, he says,

00:24:55.650 --> 00:25:02.030
 one thing you'd never do, Jim, is you never plant strawberries after broccoli. Never do that,

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:09.930
 you know? Sure enough, now that's exactly what I do. Because see, the problem was with,

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:15.840
 if you had broccoli, even when you, after you harvested the crop and you've incorporated the

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:24.070
 residue, the broccoli stems are still there to some degree. And, um, so then when you fumigate,

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:34.100
 fumigant doesn't, um, penetrate well through wherever there's a stem from, and, um, so you

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:38.980
 don't get as good a job with the chemicals, you know? Oh, geez. And so anyway, I, I had to

00:25:38.980 --> 00:25:40.500
 relearn everything, you know?

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:53.020
 Exactly what the extension agent said not to do. Yeah. Um, so farmer researcher collaborations are,

00:25:53.260 --> 00:25:59.950
 are, I'm, I'm, I'm a consultant right now for the food and agriculture organization. And one of the

00:25:59.950 --> 00:26:05.160
 projects that we're doing is like, oh, how do we actually, how do, how do these things work or not

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:15.960
 work? So what, um, it's a big question, but what made it work? Well, um, I mean, the fact is that

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:25.400
 no farmer knows everything and there's a really big benefit to having basically extension work.

00:26:25.400 --> 00:26:32.430
 And I know when I was farming in Salinas and chemically, I mean, uh, farm advisors were very

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:39.020
 helpful. Yeah. But the problem was that there was no farm advisor for organic in those days. I think,

00:26:39.020 --> 00:26:46.420
 I mean, it's really very helpful to have, for a farmer to have sort of a think group of other

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:51.620
 farmers. And, you know, the way that the system that they've worked out here is a really good one.

00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:58.450
 And they, they do a bunch of test plots with half a dozen different farms. And we meet a couple of

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:03.920
 times a year and talk about how it's going and sort of share information, even though we're competing

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:12.070
 with each other. Um, but the benefit is that we, we learn more about plant nutrition and insect ecology

00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:21.090
 because we're collaborating with everybody else. And so it improves the overall performance of our

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:27.330
 various farms. So it's, you know, it's, it's a little scary to share your secrets. You know,

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:33.120
 of course you don't share every single secret, but you know, people are seeing what you're doing.

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:38.610
 All of us who are in the organic movement really believe that it's, it's a good thing that everybody

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:46.550
 else is doing it. And we don't want to just keep it. It's just our own little private club that we are

00:27:46.550 --> 00:27:53.780
 the only ones who control, you know, so, um, we believe that it's really a good thing that everybody's

00:27:53.780 --> 00:27:58.440
 doing it. And, you know, that's why I'm happy to have the competition.

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:06.530
 I hope so. If we call it an organic movement or a food movement, right? Um, there's you and Steve have a

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:12.090
 recent piece in, and, and one of the things that you guys wrote about, let me see if I can, that the part of

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:19.170
 what made it possible is time, trust, flexibility, willingness to share knowledge, values, and belief

00:28:19.170 --> 00:28:27.590
 systems. I think that's, that set of things sounds like so much beyond what, what we talk about when we

00:28:27.590 --> 00:28:34.040
 talk about research normally. Um, there's like, there's like a, there's a relationship that had

00:28:34.040 --> 00:28:40.110
 to be built, right? Yeah. Well, it's important because it, it, um, you have to get beyond the

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:49.250
 natural tendency of competitors to be secretive about what they're doing. And so you have to believe

00:28:49.250 --> 00:28:57.600
 in a greater cause than, you know, you being able to outsmart your competitor down the road.

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:04.440
 So I think that's an important characteristic of, of the organic movement in some ways. There's,

00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:10.550
 there's a lot more sharing of information in our movement because it's, it's not just about us,

00:29:10.550 --> 00:29:17.220
 our, as an individual farmer making it, it's about increasing the amount of organic food that's

00:29:17.220 --> 00:29:25.090
 available overall. And it has increased around here. I think, I think also my article is like something

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:30.530
 about a 2000% increase in organic production in Monterey County and in Santa Cruz County. But,

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:36.670
 um, that's only between 1997 and about 2006. I mean, I'm sure it's, it's more than that.

00:29:36.670 --> 00:29:43.920
 Oh yeah. It's, it's, it's just incredible to see companies that do, you know, $50 million in sales,

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:52.300
 you know, just an individual farming operation. They do that. It's, you know, it's quite amazing.

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:58.750
 Have labor relations also changed over that time?

00:29:58.750 --> 00:30:05.650
 Well, I don't know. See, I don't really follow what everybody else is doing. So I don't really

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:12.760
 know, um, what's going on and other people's operations. But I do know that, that there are

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:18.750
 some conventional farms that have great labor practices and do, you know, provide health insurance

00:30:18.750 --> 00:30:25.010
 for their people and that sort of thing. And, um, and you know, there are other ways where there are

00:30:25.010 --> 00:30:31.940
 organic farms that are doing really a great thing and other ones that are not doing so great. I mean,

00:30:31.940 --> 00:30:38.350
 I don't know any organic farmers who are doing a terrible job, but I wouldn't be surprised if they

00:30:38.350 --> 00:30:47.420
 exist. But unfortunately I don't know. What else I've since clearly growing organic strawberries turned

00:30:47.420 --> 00:30:52.500
 out to be possible. What other, what other things do you think there are that, that people say are

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:59.680
 impossible now that you suspect might not be? Well, I think the, if one of the things that

00:30:59.680 --> 00:31:08.110
 they say is impossible is to pay better wages and to pay the union benefits. And I agree that it's

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:16.060
 difficult to do that in the current system because farmers get beat up by the chain stores that control

00:31:16.060 --> 00:31:21.260
 a lot of the bark. So they don't receive enough money for their product to be able to pay for all those

00:31:21.260 --> 00:31:28.290
 things. They might in some one year, but they might not the next year. I feel like there really needs to

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:35.710
 be a different kind of a relationship between the stores and the farmers in order to, for the farmers

00:31:35.710 --> 00:31:42.710
 to be able to pay for the extra cost. And fortunately I've had really good relationships with the stores

00:31:42.710 --> 00:31:48.740
 that I sell to. Um, and also at the farmer's market, you, you have a relationship with your customers

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:57.090
 directly. So that's, that's been helpful. But yeah, I understand it is difficult and scary for

00:31:57.090 --> 00:32:05.290
 some farmers to think about having a union contract, but I mean, I'm living proof that it worked out

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:11.340
 for me. But I think in addition to that, I sort of formed strong relationships with the stores that I

00:32:11.340 --> 00:32:18.540
 supply who support what I'm trying to do. And so that that's been helpful and not everybody can

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:26.420
 do that. So I can see where it's hard, but, um, on the other hand, it is possible. And so that's the

00:32:26.420 --> 00:32:31.500
 big thing now. I mean, you know, the organic movement has come a long ways and it's really off

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:38.500
 running on its own now, but it's, it's the labor issues that are, are lagging behind.

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:43.060
 Maybe this is kind of coming full circle, but that, uh, you've kind of introduced some

00:32:43.060 --> 00:32:48.400
 collective ownership to, to your business. Can you talk about that?

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:54.690
 It's not collective ownership. Um, it's a traditional employee stock ownership plan.

00:32:54.690 --> 00:32:55.410
 Okay.

00:32:55.410 --> 00:33:01.620
 So we have, uh, called an ESOP, employee stock ownership plan here. And the company contributes

00:33:01.620 --> 00:33:08.210
 cash or stock or both to this trust, which is a legally separate entity.

00:33:08.210 --> 00:33:16.330
 And it's, um, it's actually a retirement program. And so it owns stock in the company. It owns about

00:33:16.330 --> 00:33:22.570
 a sixth of the stock in the company right now. And it has cash. It doesn't have investments in

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:29.070
 stock other than the company stock at the moment, but it, it may wind up doing that in the future.

00:33:29.070 --> 00:33:33.560
 Although I think that investing in the company is safer than the stock market.

00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:42.870
 But you know, it's one of 11,000 companies in the United States where employees own part or

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:51.090
 all of the business. So like UPS is an ESOP Southwest airlines. A lot of big companies have

00:33:51.090 --> 00:34:01.680
 even cargo is 40% owned by its employees. Yeah. Yeah. So that was a shock to me. They have something

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:08.770
 like 160,000 employees all over the world. Yeah. And you know, they own 40% of the company,

00:34:08.770 --> 00:34:16.080
 believe it or not. But there isn't any buddy in agriculture. Cargill is not really a farming

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:23.950
 company. It's a marketing company. So we are the only actual farm that has an ESOP. It's expensive

00:34:23.950 --> 00:34:30.630
 to maintain costs about $20,000 a year to just to administer it. It's a commitment, but I believe

00:34:30.630 --> 00:34:35.630
 in it. You know, it's sort of an alternative to the co-op model. If you want to be an independent

00:34:35.630 --> 00:34:40.740
 farmer, you go be an independent farmer. If you want to be part of a cooperative, you go join a co-op.

00:34:40.740 --> 00:34:47.380
 If you want to have a little stake in the ownership and hopefully a decent place to work,

00:34:47.380 --> 00:34:51.940
 then you come and work here. So we don't promise to be everything to everybody.

00:34:52.660 --> 00:34:58.610
 We're more of a traditionally managed business, which a lot of workers prefer. They don't want

00:34:58.610 --> 00:35:02.930
 to have somebody asking them stuff all the time. You know, what do we do about this? What do we do

00:35:02.930 --> 00:35:08.980
 about that? They don't want to take the responsibility for it. But on the other hand, we try to be what

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:15.250
 you might call enlightened managers, where, you know, you ask people who are in the field,

00:35:15.250 --> 00:35:21.700
 what do they think about this? What do they think about that? A lot of almost all the interactions

00:35:21.700 --> 00:35:27.940
 that I have with people is, what do you think about this or that? What do you think about the

00:35:27.940 --> 00:35:34.270
 watering we're doing? And do you think that these strawberries are going to be over ripe by Monday?

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:40.730
 What do you think? We should pick them today or wait until Monday? Or, you know, is this the right

00:35:40.730 --> 00:35:45.410
 tractor implement do you think that we're using? And that sort of thing. And people have opinions about

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:52.700
 that. And so they like being asked about those things because, you know, we don't always do what

00:35:52.700 --> 00:36:01.340
 it is that they recommend, but it's part of the mix and their opinion counts. I mean, they, and you

00:36:01.340 --> 00:36:04.780
 know, if they feel like it's not a good idea to do something somewhere, they say, no, that's,

00:36:04.780 --> 00:36:09.200
 hey, Jim, that's, this is not a good idea. You know, we should do it the other way around,

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:14.320
 you know, which is, you know, it's nothing new in the world of management. I mean, you know,

00:36:14.320 --> 00:36:16.080
 a lot of companies work that way.

00:36:16.080 --> 00:36:21.760
 So much of farming or what I've heard from a lot of farmers that I've worked for this is it's that

00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:26.040
 close observation that, yeah, that, that matters.

00:36:27.080 --> 00:36:32.040
 Yeah. I mean, you, you just have to pay attention. I mean, and that, you know, you just look at stuff.

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:38.260
 I mean, I, I am not a scientist, you know, and I can, I just look at a plant and I say, well,

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:46.460
 that plant's not happy. What, what, what am I doing wrong there? And so it is, it's about

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:54.600
 paying attention. So now I, I'm sort of semi-retired. I have two managers, one in production and one in

00:36:55.320 --> 00:37:04.230
 farm sales. And, um, I just check in every day, look at all the fields and talk to people in the

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:10.610
 field and in the store and farmers market people and customers. And which reminds me,

00:37:10.610 --> 00:37:18.800
 I need to go out and get some peas later today. So, you know, my job at this point is to be more

00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:25.370
 of a mentor because I'm 68, you know, I can't do this same physical stuff that I could. After I was

00:37:25.370 --> 00:37:30.730
 60, I sort of, that was the point where I couldn't do stuff so much anymore because you get sort of

00:37:30.730 --> 00:37:36.780
 beat up to doing this. Yeah. And I know you're also thinking about changing a bigger food system

00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:40.300
 at the same time as a part of your retirement. I know you, I've heard you talk about regional

00:37:40.300 --> 00:37:46.220
 food hubs and about labor politics. Um, what, where are you putting your energy in terms of that right

00:37:46.220 --> 00:37:53.820
 now? Well, there, what I'm doing is trying to do a similar thing that I did with at the farm is

00:37:53.820 --> 00:38:01.030
 actually, um, doing it ourselves rather than trying to convince other people that they should

00:38:01.030 --> 00:38:07.260
 change their, what they're doing and come along and join us and we'll all work together and say, no,

00:38:07.260 --> 00:38:15.790
 no, no, it's more like we'll create something. And, um, if people like it, they'll come to it. You

00:38:15.790 --> 00:38:23.350
 know, what are you creating? Well, we're trying to create a, um, vertically integrated, um,

00:38:23.990 --> 00:38:33.700
 system over in Fresno where, which grows food processes, it distributes it, wholesale, some of

00:38:33.700 --> 00:38:42.220
 it, but mostly retails. So, um, it's, that's what we do here at the farm is we, we're completely

00:38:42.220 --> 00:38:49.090
 vertically and we do everything soup to nuts. In fact, I think some large percentage of our sales are

00:38:49.090 --> 00:38:56.820
 direct to the public retail. It's like 75% maybe, you know, so that's a lot. That's the way,

00:38:56.820 --> 00:39:03.380
 the way I'm able to afford to pay for the benefits because, you know, we get the full retail price.

00:39:03.380 --> 00:39:10.990
 So that's been an important part of the business model. And so that's what, what I am been saying

00:39:10.990 --> 00:39:20.250
 about, um, how some kids are coming in with some strawberries that they picked. Yeah.

00:39:21.850 --> 00:39:26.130
 Showing grandma and grandpa. Grandma and grandpa are appropriately thrilled.

00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:36.620
 They're contributing to that direct sale. I think I will too with some, uh, strawberry shortcake and,

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:40.540
 but thanks so much for talking, Jim. I really appreciate it. Yeah.

00:39:40.540 --> 00:39:45.250
 My pleasure. Thank you for doing what you're doing because I think it's super important that,

00:39:45.250 --> 00:39:51.410
 that people, um, think about the full range of things that are involved in farming and it's,

00:39:52.060 --> 00:39:56.570
 you know, there's a lot, a lot of people things that happen in farming.

00:39:56.570 --> 00:40:07.510
 Thanks so much.

